<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">People's Socialist Republic of Albania</h1><p class="st-meta">1946 CE – 1991 CE · capital Tirana</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Enver Hoxha's communist Albania, the most isolated state in Europe, which broke in turn with Yugoslavia, the Soviet Union and China and banned all religion.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enver-hoxha">Enver Hoxha's</a> Communist-led Partisans took power as German forces withdrew in November 1944 from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/albanian-kingdom-1943-1944">Albanian Kingdom</a> (1943-1944). After elections with a single list in December 1945, the monarchy was formally abolished and the People's <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/albania">Republic of Albania</a> proclaimed on 11 January 1946, with Hoxha as leader. Opponents were tried, executed or imprisoned.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The regime was strictly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stalinism">Stalinist</a>: land was collectivised, industry nationalised and the Sigurimi secret police watched everyone. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enver-hoxha">Hoxha</a> broke with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> in 1948, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> in 1961 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in 1978, leaving <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/albania">Albania</a> almost alone. In 1967 it declared itself the world's first atheist state, closing all mosques and churches, and built hundreds of thousands of concrete bunkers. It was renamed the People's Socialist Republic in 1976.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enver-hoxha">Hoxha</a> died in 1985, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramiz-alia">Ramiz Alia</a> allowed cautious change. Student protests and mass flight abroad in 1990 forced the legalisation of opposition parties, and after the first multi-party elections the country was renamed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/albania">Republic of Albania</a> in April 1991.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/peoples-socialist-republic-of-albania.jpg" alt="People's Socialist Republic of Albania" width="320" height="229"><figcaption>Image: Sadik Kaceli · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Albania_(1946%E2%80%931992).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1946 CE – 1991 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tirana</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Enver Hoxha, Mehmet Shehu, Ramiz Alia</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>State atheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Albanian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/albania">Albania</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>People's Republic of Albania, Communist Albania</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1968&amp;focus=peoples-socialist-republic-of-albania" aria-label="People's Socialist Republic of Albania on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5164 704 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where People's Socialist Republic of Albania lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5484 960L5484 960M5484 960L5484 960"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5484 960h0M5484 960h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5484 960h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5484 960h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Tirana. <a href="/atlas?year=1968&amp;focus=peoples-socialist-republic-of-albania">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
